<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marche</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as the Marches</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Marche is a region of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> and the Adriatic Sea, with its capital at Ancona. Most of it belonged to the Papal States from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> until 1860, when Piedmontese troops occupied it on their way south to meet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a>, and a plebiscite joined it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a>. Urbino, seat of the Montefeltro court and birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a>, lies in the region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marche.jpg" alt="Marche" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS さららはりさららららりりささらりりさらさらしさりらさらさりるさりさらりらららさららは さりさしさしさしらラ・フランス · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marches_in_Italy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
